<interrogationes unitas="internamentum">

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I intern strings so I can compare them by pointer?
    <responsum symbolum="chorda_internare chorda_internare_ex_literis internamentum_globale">chorda_internare
      (or chorda_internare_ex_literis for a C string) returns a chorda*
      to the canonical copy; interning the same bytes again through the
      SAME interner returns the same pointer, so two interned names are
      equal exactly when the pointers are equal.
      chorda_internare_ex_literis looks the literal up before
      allocating, so repeats cost nothing.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I intern the empty string?
    <responsum symbolum="chorda_internare chorda_internare_ex_literis" via="lib/internamentum.worklog.md">No:
      both functions return NIHIL for a zero-length chorda or "",
      because the hash table refuses empty keys. This is a written
      contract, not a bug: actio, insula and stml treat that NIHIL as
      "empty name", and a 2026-09-22 attempt to change it broke them.
      If "" must be a real value, keep your own canonical empty chorda
      (json and componens do).</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I choose between internamentum_globale and my own interner?
    <responsum symbolum="internamentum_globale internamentum_creare">internamentum_globale
      is a lazily created singleton with its own dynamic piscina
      (64 KB to start) that is never freed; use it when several
      libraries must share pointers. internamentum_creare(piscina)
      makes a private one whose copies live and die with that piscina.
      Pointers from two different interners never compare equal, even
      for the same bytes.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I free the memory of interned strings with internamentum_vacare?
    <responsum symbolum="internamentum_vacare tabula_dispersa_vacare piscina_destruere">No.
      internamentum_vacare only empties the lookup table; the copies
      stay in the piscina, so old pointers still read correctly but are
      no longer canonical: interning the same bytes afterwards gives a
      NEW pointer that does not equal the old one. Memory comes back
      only when the interner's piscina is destroyed or reset.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can chorda_est_internata tell me whether my pointer is the canonical copy?
    <responsum symbolum="chorda_est_internata chorda_internare">No:
      it looks the BYTES up, so it is VERUM for any chorda with the same
      content as an interned one, canonical or not. To get the
      canonical pointer, intern the chorda again with chorda_internare
      (no new copy is made when it is already present).</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

</interrogationes>
